WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.127
Before we start, choose the answers that best describe you.

00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:07.841
First, how much experience do you have with AI?

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:16.508
You may be exploring for the first time, using a chat assistant occasionally, using
it daily, or already building AI applications.

00:00:16.508 --> 00:00:19.397
Second, what do you use it for?

00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:23.523
Think about writing, finding information, coding, analysis, operations, or learning.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:26.000
Third, what do you expect today?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.253
Would you prefer a live build from an empty project, guided examples with an
explanation, or time to build along?

00:00:34.253 --> 00:00:36.317
There is no correct answer.

00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:40.444
These questions help the presenter choose the pace and vocabulary.

00:00:40.444 --> 00:00:45.396
Do not enter a name, email, employer, account, tenant, or customer detail.

00:00:45.396 --> 00:00:47.460
Read the poll's response-handling notice.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.825
A response held in one browser is not a shared live audience tally.

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:57.777
We never display a simulated result as though the audience submitted it.

00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:23.833
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.802
Here is the app definition.

00:01:25.802 --> 00:01:28.951
We want an inbox containing three synthetic tickets.

00:01:28.951 --> 00:01:36.824
A user can search by title or service, filter by priority, select a ticket, and
create a new invented ticket.

00:01:36.824 --> 00:01:39.579
The detail view shows the selected record.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:45.091
An Ask AI panel has a clear initial state: AI is not connected yet.

00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:46.665
That last sentence matters.

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:52.570
The first build must not display a canned answer or pretend that a backend exists.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:58.081
The same definition can go into Lovable, v zero, Base forty four, or Codex.

00:01:58.081 --> 00:01:59.262
The builder changes.

00:01:59.262 --> 00:02:01.230
The acceptance contract does not.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:11.859
In the comparisons later in this recording, we test the visible contract and explain
the actual backend limits rather than assuming that similar interfaces have
equivalent security.

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.567
Our first finish line is an ordinary working app.

00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:16.701
Create one ticket.

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:18.590
Find it by its title.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.123
Combine that search with a different priority and inspect the empty result.

00:02:23.123 --> 00:02:26.902
Clear the search and filter, then select the original ticket.

00:02:26.902 --> 00:02:31.436
Only after those controls work do we add the real AI backend.

00:02:31.436 --> 00:02:37.859
Our second finish line is an authenticated, bounded investigation that uses the
permitted tools and preserves uncertainty.

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:45.793
Our third is an observable operation, with a run in the app and a separately matched
trace in the intended project.

00:02:45.793 --> 00:02:53.727
Local tests, a green build, and an exporter acknowledgement each prove something
useful, but none automatically proves the other finish lines.

00:02:53.727 --> 00:02:57.127
Keep these evidence classes separate in your own notes.

00:02:57.127 --> 00:03:03.928
If a provider, secret, or runtime capability is missing, write that down and keep
the missing gate unverified.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:30.000
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.
